>
>The structure of lojban tanru is like yyyy-mm-dd:  central part in the
>end, delimiting peripherals prepended (or elided) as far as necessitated
>by the context.

A date is not a tanru; it is more similar to a name.  You seem to be 
arguing on the basis of restriction, and Lojban restricts using relative 
clauses/phrases (poi/pe/po/po'u) which can go either before or after a 
sumti but are less marked after the sumti.  In general, restrictive 
information goes later in the sentence (tanru being an exception, but 
turning a tanru into a relative clause also moves the restriction to the 
right in default).

